- the complete plan is persisted to a decision file (`bus.c.<gid>.json`) before the group is flushed, and each request's outcome — its acks or its exact rejection — is written to `bus.r.<token>.json` only after the commit is durable, so a request drained by another process, or after a crash mid-flush, is adopted with its original result.

The merged state is kept between drains by an incremental reader of the active log tail (keyed by device/inode and the log's first-line fingerprint), so the write path never does the full directory walk; compaction and truncation (which replace the head) transparently fall back to one full scan. The epoch credential is re-validated at every pipeline boundary (lock entry, gather, decision, each flush chunk, commit, settlement): a holder taken over while queued or mid group fails its whole request, and its group either never commits or is filtered wholesale by the epoch fence — no stale byte mixes into the log. A write or fsync failure rolls the entire group back physically, leaving the bus byte-for-byte as it was. A crash leaves either the complete group or none of it: the uncommitted bracket/torn tail is severed and the decision reconciles the survivors on the next open.

## Read path

Reads are decoupled from the write chain. `readRange`, `read`, `stats()`, `usage()`, and the read half of `replay`/position lookups serve from a dedicated **read-only view** that is refreshed without taking `bus.lock` and without walking the directory:

- ordinary appends are caught by an incremental reader of the active-log tail (device/inode plus first-line fingerprint), so fetching a page near the tail costs O(new bytes since the last look plus the page), never O(log length);
- a snapshot switch (compaction/truncate), a fence, or a writer caught mid group is detected by a changed inode/head or an open bracket, and transparently falls back to one *non-destructive* `scanDirectory` — it never heals, unlinks or otherwise mutates, so readers can run while writers continuously commit and never queue behind a drain;
- every view is an immutable **committed prefix**: a commit group is visible whole or not at all, sequence numbers are continuous with no repeats, stats/positions only move forward, and two reads of the same range straddling a snapshot switch return the same surviving records;
- the view is an independent copy of the write state, so a drain advancing the write counters never leaks a half group to readers;
- a taken-over (fenced) instance keeps reading exactly as before — only its writes/truncations fail, as before — and reads remain available after `close()`, served from the last materialized view with no side effects.

Only position *writes* (`register` of a new name, a forward `advance`, the implicit registration on first `read`) still take the write lock, where the no-reset / no-backward decision is re-adjudicated against the merged position; the lookups themselves stay lock-free.

